        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "Its name was Livyatan. And it might have been one of the most dangerous animals the ocean has ever produced."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s02_01",
      "scene": 2,
      "scene_title": "Intro",
      "start_sec": 31.248,
      "end_sec": 46.906,
      "duration_sec": 15.658,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s02_01.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": null,
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s02_01.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 15.308,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "LIVYATAN",
      "narration": "Today we're going back about thirteen million years, to the Miocene epoch, to meet an animal that paleontologists think sat at the very top of its ecosystem. We'll look at what it was, how big it got, what it hunted, and why it disappeared."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s02_02",
      "scene": 2,
      "scene_title": "Intro",
      "start_sec": 46.906,
      "end_sec": 59.533,
      "duration_sec": 12.627,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s02_02.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": null,
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s02_02.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 12.277,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "Let's start with the name, because it's a bit of a story. The scientific name is Livyatan melvillei. The first part comes from the Hebrew word \"Leviathan,\" a sea monster from ancient texts."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s02_03",
      "scene": 2,
      "scene_title": "Intro",
      "start_sec": 59.533,
      "end_sec": 68.556,
      "duration_sec": 9.023,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s02_03.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": {
        "path": "clips/s02_03.mp4",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s02_03.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 8.673,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "slow_pan",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "The second part honors Herman Melville, the author of Moby-Dick. So the animal is named after a sea monster and the man who wrote about one."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s03_01",
      "scene": 3,
