        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 10.919,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "That's an important detail. This wasn't a creature of one isolated sea. It lived in the warm, productive waters of the ancient Pacific, where many marine animals were thriving."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s05_03",
      "scene": 5,
      "scene_title": "Where It Lived",
      "start_sec": 168.881,
      "end_sec": 180.072,
      "duration_sec": 11.191,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s05_03.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": null,
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s05_03.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 10.841,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "Its neighbors would have included other whales, sharks, and seabirds. Some of those animals were big. Some were dangerous. And Livyatan sat near the top of the menu."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s06_01",
      "scene": 6,
      "scene_title": "Diet",
      "start_sec": 180.072,
      "end_sec": 193.013,
      "duration_sec": 12.941,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s06_01.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": {
        "path": "clips/s06_01.mp4",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s06_01.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 12.591,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "slow_pan",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "So what did Livyatan eat? This is where we have to be careful. Scientists don't have a video recording from 13 million years ago, so what we know comes from fossils and comparisons with living animals."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s06_02",
      "scene": 6,
      "scene_title": "Diet",
      "start_sec": 193.013,
      "end_sec": 206.294,
      "duration_sec": 13.281,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s06_02.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": null,
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s06_02.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 12.931,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "Hypothesis",
      "narration": "One line of evidence is bite-like marks found on the bones of other ancient whales. If those marks really came from Livyatan, it means the animal was hunting other large marine mammals, possibly baleen whales."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s06_03",
      "scene": 6,
      "scene_title": "Diet",
      "start_sec": 206.294,
      "end_sec": 223.571,
      "duration_sec": 17.277,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s06_03.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": {
        "path": "clips/s06_03.mp4",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s06_03.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 16.927,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "parallax",
      "overlay_text": "Hypothesis",
      "narration": "Could it also have eaten large fish and squid? Possibly. But the strongest evidence, as far as we know, points toward other whales. That's the part that makes Livyatan so unusual. It may have been one of the few animals in its ocean that could take on prey its own size."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s07_01",
      "scene": 7,
      "scene_title": "Compare To Today",
      "start_sec": 223.571,
      "end_sec": 241.24,
      "duration_sec": 17.669,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s07_01.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": null,
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s07_01.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 17.319,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "Livyatan is related to the sperm whale we have today. But the two aren't identical. Modern sperm whales have teeth mostly on the lower jaw, and they're famous for diving deep to hunt squid. Livyatan had large teeth in its upper jaw too, which is one reason it looked so different."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s07_02",
      "scene": 7,
      "scene_title": "Compare To Today",
      "start_sec": 241.24,
      "end_sec": 246.841,
      "duration_sec": 5.601,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s07_02.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": null,
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s07_02.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 5.251,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "Here's the honest picture. Modern sperm whales are fearsome deep-sea hunters,"
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s07_03",
      "scene": 7,
      "scene_title": "Compare To Today",
      "start_sec": 246.841,
      "end_sec": 257.457,
      "duration_sec": 10.616,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s07_03.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": {
        "path": "clips/s07_03.mp4",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s07_03.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 10.266,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "slow_pan",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "but Livyatan was a different kind of predator. It may have been more of a surface and mid-water hunter, going after big prey in an ocean that was warmer and busier than today's."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s08_01",
      "scene": 8,
      "scene_title": "The End Of The Giant",
      "start_sec": 257.457,
      "end_sec": 271.678,
      "duration_sec": 14.221,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s08_01.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": {
        "path": "clips/s08_01.mp4",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s08_01.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 13.871,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "slow_push_in",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "Livyatan disappeared around five million years ago. No single cause has been proven. Scientists point to changes in ocean temperature, shifts in prey populations, and changes in ocean food webs as the likely factors."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s08_02",
      "scene": 8,
      "scene_title": "The End Of The Giant",
      "start_sec": 271.678,
      "end_sec": 281.197,
      "duration_sec": 9.519,
      "image": {
        "path": "images/s08_02.jpg",
        "status": "ready"
      },
      "clip": null,
      "audio": {
        "path": "voiceover/s08_02.mp3",
        "status": "ready",
        "duration_sec": 9.169,
        "words": []
      },
      "motion": "none",
      "overlay_text": "",
      "narration": "What's striking is how much it teaches us about how fragile giant predators can be. Even the biggest animals in the ocean depend on the systems around them."
    },
    {
      "shot_id": "s09_01",
